{
    "title": "Continuation of the National Emergency With Respect to the Global Illicit Drug Trade",
    "type": "Presidential Document",
    "date": "December 12, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-12/pdf/2025-22786.pdf",
    "summary": "The White House has extended the national emergency targeting the global illicit drug trade for another year. First declared in 2021, it will now continue past December 15, 2025, due to ongoing threats from fentanyl and other synthetic opioids that the administration says endanger the country\u2019s security, foreign policy, and economy.\n\nThe notice cites the continued toll of overdose deaths and non\u2011fatal overdoses, the role of drug cartels and other criminal networks, the spread of highly potent drugs, and the growing use of the internet to sell them. By keeping the emergency in place, the government preserves special tools created under a 2021 executive order to disrupt the production, financing, and distribution of illegal drugs and their chemical inputs.\n\nCommunities most affected by overdoses, families who have lost loved ones, first responders, and public health teams may benefit if the extension helps slow the flow of dangerous substances and reduces violence tied to trafficking. Law enforcement and policymakers also retain flexibility to act quickly against suppliers, intermediaries, and online sales channels.\n\nAt the same time, some groups may have concerns. Civil liberties advocates and good\u2011government watchers often question the long use of emergency powers and call for clear limits and oversight. Banks, shippers, and online platforms may face added compliance burdens and enforcement risk. International partners and legitimate businesses handling chemicals or pharmaceuticals could encounter stricter scrutiny. And some public health experts warn that a heavy focus on enforcement, without matching investments in treatment and harm reduction, may not reduce demand or prevent deaths.\n\nThe notice will be published in the Federal Register and sent to Congress. What matters next is how these authorities are used in practice and whether they are paired with prevention, treatment, and recovery efforts that address the crisis from all sides.",
